# GitHub feature workflow

Default delivery path: **feature branch → PR → `main`**. Every feature branch has a matching GitHub issue. All issue/PR/branch text is **English**.

## Naming

```
feature/<issue-number>-<slug-from-issue-title>
```

- `<issue-number>` = GitHub issue number (e.g. `8`)
- `<slug-from-issue-title>` = issue title adapted to a short kebab-case slug (lowercase, hyphens, no punctuation)

Examples: issue `#8` “Add FFmpeg transcode pipeline” → `feature/8-add-ffmpeg-transcode-pipeline`.

## When on `main` and the user asks for a **new** change

**Propose** first (do not start until they confirm):

1. Create a GitHub issue in `JRealValdes/jarvis-media-processor`
2. Create the associated feature branch from up-to-date `main` and develop there

Then follow [`.cursor/skills/github-feature-flow/SKILL.md`](../skills/github-feature-flow/SKILL.md).

## When on `main` and the user asks to solve / work on issue `#X`

Do **not** create a new issue. Proceed (no need to re-propose the full flow):

1. Read the issue with `gh issue view X` (title, body, checklist).
2. Check out the existing development branch for `#X`, or create it if missing (`feature/X-<slug>` from up-to-date `main`).
3. Develop there against the issue requirements; expand the issue body when useful.

Details: same skill file, section “Work on an existing issue”.

## Already on a feature branch

- Implement the requested changes on the current branch.
- When new scope genuinely belongs on the same issue, **expand the issue body** (English) without asking — only when it adds clear value; do not spam trivial edits.

## When the task is done and committed

As soon as the issue work is **complete**, verified, and **all related changes are committed** on the feature branch, **proactively propose** the delivery close-out (do not wait for the user to ask):

1. **Push** the feature branch to `origin`
2. **Open a PR** into `main` (title ends with `(#X)`; body links `Closes #X` when the issue is fully done)
3. **Squash-merge** the PR into `main` (after checks are green / user is satisfied) — always `gh pr merge --squash`, never a merge commit
4. **Branch cleanup** — delete remote feature branch if it remains, delete local feature branch, check out `main`, pull/prune

Propose this chain in one short message. **Do not** push, open the PR, merge, or delete branches until the user confirms (they may confirm the whole chain at once).

## Pull requests

- Title ends with a space and `(#X)` where `X` is the issue number, e.g. `Add FFmpeg transcode pipeline (#8)`.
- Link the PR to the issue as development work (`Closes #X` or Development sidebar / `gh` linking). Body in English.
- Open a PR when the user confirms the close-out proposal above (or explicitly asks for a PR).

## After merge — branch cleanup

Once a PR is **merged**, remove the feature branch as part of the confirmed close-out (or propose cleanup immediately if merge happened without it):

1. **Remote (`origin`)** — delete if still present (`git push origin --delete feature/<N>-…` or `gh` equivalent), when the user confirmed cleanup.
2. **Local** — switch to `main`, then delete the local feature branch (`git branch -d feature/<N>-…`). Prune stale remote-tracking refs (`git fetch --prune`) when useful.
3. Leave the working tree on `main` and **`git pull`** (or `git pull origin main`) so local `main` matches the merge just landed.

Prefer `git branch -d` (safe delete). After squash-merge, Git may claim the branch is “not fully merged”; if the PR is merged and the user confirmed cleanup, `git branch -D` is OK.

## Merge strategy

PRs into `main` are **always squash-merged** (`gh pr merge <PR> --squash`). Do not use `--merge` or `--rebase` unless the user explicitly asks otherwise.

## Out of scope here

Commit message format stays Conventional Commits (see incremental-commits rules). Do not push, open PRs, merge, or delete branches without user confirmation.
